 "title": "Battle of Manzikert",
 "date_label": "26 August 1071",
 "year": 1071,
 "intro": "In August 1071 the Byzantine emperor Romanos IV Diogenes led a large, mixed army to the eastern frontier near Lake Van to settle the question of Turkic raiding and settlement. He met the Seljuk sultan Alp Arslan at Manzikert, in the highlands north of the lake. The Byzantine force had been halved by a detachment sent towards Khliat, and its Turkic mercenaries were wavering. Over several days the Seljuk horse archers refused a pitched fight, drew the Byzantines forward, and on 26 August caught them in a disordered retreat. The emperor was captured and the professional core of the army destroyed.",
 "outcome": "Seljuk victory: Romanos IV was captured and the Byzantine field army broken, opening Anatolia to Turkic settlement.",
 "uncertainty": "Sources disagree on army sizes (Byzantine figures range from about 40,000 to over 200,000) and give no compass bearings or exact positions. The directions, the line of advance, the Seljuk crescent and the Seljuk camp are reconstructed and marked conjectural. The fate of Tarchaniotes' detachment is unclear, and accounts differ on who fled first.",
